ocated; it adds dispatched, vehicle_available, dispatch_slot_available. It does not remove dispatch_allocated. So dispatch_allocated stays true. So we could allocate, dispatch, then allocate again (since dispatch_slot_available becomes true after dispatch). However the allocate action's precondition requires not dispatch_allocated ?candidate (for that candidate). After dispatch, dispatch_allocated remains true, so we cannot allocate again for same candidate. But we need to allocate for different candidates; each candidate's dispatch_allocated must be true. So we can allocate for origin_availability_alpha, dispatch it, then allocate for destination_availability_alpha, dispatch it, then allocate for shift_candidate_beta, dispatch it. After each dispatch, dispatch_slot_available becomes true again, allowing next allocation. However the allocate action's precondition also requires dispatch_ready ?candidate. After we dispatch, the candidate becomes dispatched, but dispatch_ready may still be true? Not removed. The effect does not remove dispatch_ready. So dispatch_ready stays true. That's fine.)
